    "turn_text": "Well, when I was born, my father was in Congress from Baltimore. And when I was seven years old, in first grade, he became the mayor of Baltimore. And so when I went away to college, he was still the mayor of Baltimore. It was the only life we really knew, as a toddler or even my first memories, of course. Well since it's going off to Washington and I remember my first visit to the Capitol as a little girl with my five older brothers It's always been about public service really not about politics but public service the we always had this sense of responsibility to other people and we lived in it and little Italy in Baltimore, Maryland we always lived there and People thought when he became mayor he'd moved someplace else, but that was really home for us and So since I was a little girl, I could, people would knock on the door or call and I could remember what my mother would say and I could tell people who to call to get a bed and city hospital, go on welfare, get a place to live in the projects. I mean, it was just part of what we did. Did you go",
